From da6abfb8f823b10d5c5d9fadc94eb522cbb11d72 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 16:39:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(blocks): apply native custom block shapes and lighting

Read per-connection custom block metadata from ViaBedrock for reserved
carrier states. Apply separate collision and selection shapes, occlusion,
support, suffocation, brightness and light values on the client. Preserve
ordinary block behavior when no custom definition owns a state.

Semantic shape tests cover holes, disabled collision and boxes extending
above one block. Runtime replay exercises the hooks without public joins.

Water-free redstone-wire carriers expand the core pool without consuming
ordinary Bedrock wire mappings. Suppress their Java ambient redstone
particles and use constant white tint sources for block and terrain
particle colors only while custom metadata owns the state.

Honor explicit custom material face_dimming=false in flat and ambient
occlusion rendering. Java 26.3 uses an upward model override, but Nether
cardinal lighting still dims that direction to 0.9. Restore multiplier 1
only for registered custom carriers with that override. Preserve ordinary
states, world light, emission and ambient occlusion. Decode generated mixed
face models through the Java cuboid loader in a focused integration test.
The target Hive protocol 2193 capture contains 136 disabled material instances.

Load protocol-bound physical properties from the accepted converted server
pack when ViaBedrock runs in ViaProxy. Use the same collision, selection,
lighting and carrier integration as direct connections. Keep properties
through asynchronous reloads, replace them atomically, clear on disconnect
and prevent a stale prepared reload from restoring a previous session.
Local resource packs cannot override this server-owned mapping.

Targeted tests cover server-pack precedence, shape and light values,
reload ordering, removal and disconnect during preparation. The native
VoxelShapes registry represents rendering culling and remains separate
from the physical boxes transported here.

Retain native rule annotations through Java cuboid deserialization and
terrain quad baking. Evaluate authored neighbor directions, identity,
permutation, layer and voxel face coverage before submitting each terrain
quad. Keep inventory drawing outside neighbor culling. Read both physical
properties and culling definitions from the same accepted server-pack
lifecycle on direct and ViaProxy connections.

Use distinct annotated quad instances so Java's geometry/material
interning cannot merge different native rules. Numerical tests cover
condition independence, opaque-neighbor opt-out, opposite participation
and holes in coverage. Vanilla partial-block mapping and native rendering
comparisons remain required.

Apply core position offsets to terrain, outlines and the continuous camera
envelope while keeping native floored movement collision separate. Java's
edge and corner pruning previously skipped shifted shapes and caused
repeated strict-BDS corrections. Retain boundary cells for custom shapes
that can extend into them, without changing ordinary block classification.
Direct and ViaProxy fixtures verify two-axis and three-axis landings and
actual injected shape methods before and after resource reload.

Clear session metadata after Minecraft's central disconnect finishes.
Local leave bypasses the packet-listener disconnect callback, whereas
clearClientLevel belongs to server reconfiguration and must retain the
accepted metadata. Preserve intentionally retained server packs during
transfers and invalidate stale prepared reload results on ordinary leave.

Retain per-face numeric AO strength alongside culling rules through model
deserialization and distinct quad baking. Bedrock 1.26.51.1 raises the
product of corner ambient light and directional dimming to the material
exponent, before tint and world light. Apply that calculation before Java
packs vertex colors, then suppress the redundant directional multiplier.
Mixed zero/nonzero faces retain separate settings; flat terrain honors
the exponent without introducing neighbor AO. Shared face annotations
keep both connection routes and resource reloads on the same path.

Retain the native maximum of each light channel at custom terrain corners.
Java averages these samples and replaces zeros before blending, which
changes native torch and sky gradients even when chunk light is correct.
The shared core helper matches 12,288 native heterogeneous corner probes.
Keep per-vertex interpolation and source neighborhood selection separate
from this verified corner calculation; ordinary Java terrain is unchanged.

Zero ambient strength still takes four corner light samples in both native
terrain routines. Route annotated zero-strength custom faces through
corner preparation even when Java selects flat lighting. The material
exponent removes ambient and directional attenuation while preserving
native block/sky maxima. Ordinary states and unannotated faces retain the
original flat renderer and caller-provided light coordinates.

A private pinned executable fixture compares both native routines across
1,920 path pairs and 7,680 corners, including zero-strength materials.
World lookup and full visible terrain comparisons remain open parity work.

Shade all four ambient corners before interpolation on partial faces.
The pinned native vertex routine interpolates already shaded RGBA, so
applying the exponent to the weighted ambient average changes nonlinear
material gradients. Target both corner preparation branches and require
exactly two matching float stores per corner in the pinned Java renderer.
Preserve unannotated quads and ordinary Java carriers.

Original native interpolation and packing instructions distinguish this
ordering in 8,393 of 18,816 controlled probes. The fixture supplies corner
colors and does not establish native world lookup or visible parity.

Use core nested interpolation for annotated partial terrain faces. Map
Java's four ambient averages from its face adjacency order to the target
native inner and outer axes, then pack the resulting vertex color. Both
horizontal faces interpolate X before Z; Z faces interpolate X before Y;
X faces interpolate Z before Y. Full unit faces retain identical
corner colors. Ordinary states and unannotated quads keep original packing.

Complete native face emission fixtures validate all six directions with
supplied cached colors and local cuboid bounds. The previous single-face
reference generalized its arithmetic to other axes, so it overstated the
residual mismatch. Native world lookup, assignment of neighbor colors,
alternative terrain renderers, and visible comparisons remain required.

Evaluate authored vertex coordinates even when Java classifies a face as
full. Expanded cuboids and tiny insets still require native interpolation;
Java's partial-face flag does not cover those bounds. Clamp the result
after interpolation as the native color packer does.

Retain each native corner light coordinate on inset cuboid faces. The
pinned native face emitters interpolate ambient color across authored
bounds but submit unweighted corner light. Java blends the four packed
corners again on partial faces, which changes torch and sky gradients.
Retain these coordinates on registered annotated custom faces alongside
shared core maxima; ordinary Java blending remains unchanged.

Original native emission executes all six directions and seven bounds
with distinct light channels, including expanded and tiny-inset faces.
All 168 submitted light coordinates retain their corner identity.
Native runtime block flags remain a separate requirement.

Route annotated ambient faces through the shared core neighborhood sampler.
Remove the previous Java local-index and interpolation repair hooks: Java
selects a different source light and tests diagonal blockers one step
farther out. Sample native planes and preserve both ambient interpolation
and unweighted physical corner light. Read world channels with emission
applied only to block light, avoiding Java's sky emission floor.

Original 1.26.51.1 instructions validate 12,288 complete corner selections
and the 0.0005/0.9995 plane tolerances. Cached solidity/per-type ambient
identity, rotated-face behavior and visible native scenes remain unverified.

Retain native face lighting through Sodium's reusable quads, and adapt its
four light and shade outputs to the same core sampler as vanilla. Clear
annotations on raw loads and resets, retain them on baked import, and copy
them with geometry. Delegate unannotated or ordinary Java states unchanged.

Sodium 0.9.2 on Java 26.3 bypasses vanilla BlockModelLighter. Optional typed
mixin bridges avoid requiring Sodium at compile time and preserve its later
emissive, tint, normal and buffer processing, including Iris encoder hooks.
Do not replace Iris pipelines or shader selection. Shared adapter tests
cover physical corner order, inset emission and authored shade direction.
The core numeric sampler remains the owner of native lighting calculations.

Compatibility is checked with Iris 1.11.7 and Complementary Reimagined
r5.9.3 under an isolated Mesa renderer. Native propagation, real block
identity and visible native GPU parity remain separate requirements.

Prepare every accepted native material through the shared corner sampler
when Java selects flat model lighting. The target cached and uncached
corner routines agree across all five tested strengths; Java model AO
selection must not silently replace nonzero native material sampling.
Remove the obsolete flat directional-expression hook. Ordinary states
and unannotated faces retain Java behavior. Native global graphics-setting
dispatch remains a separate verification requirement.
